from expand_common import *
from functools import reduce
F=Fraction
f=family
# Nine logic families: quantifiers, model counting, canonical forms and expressivity.
t='d_logic'
f(t,'equivalence-components','Equalities followed by one implication',r'There are $n+2$ Boolean variables. The constraints are $x_1\Leftrightarrow x_2$ and $x_2\Rightarrow x_3$; all other variables are free. Count satisfying assignments.',r'$3\cdot2^{n-1}$',[r'$2^{n+1}$',r'$2^n$',r'$3\cdot2^n$'],lambda n:3*2**(n-1),[lambda n:2**(n+1),lambda n:2**n,lambda n:3*2**n],r'Identify $x_1$ with $x_2$. The allowed pair values of $(x_2,x_3)$ are 00, 01 and 11. The remaining $n-1$ variables are independently free, so multiply three allowed pairs by their Boolean choices. Do not multiply by an extra choice for $x_1$.','Adding another equality between two previously free variables generally halves the count.',['An equality always doubles the count.','The implication leaves all four pair assignments legal.','Changing an implication to equivalence never changes the model count.'],'An equality replaces two independent bits by one bit. In this model there are at least two free variables. Requiring those bits to agree removes one independent binary choice; the constrained three-variable component is unchanged.')
f(t,'nonempty-witness','Existence without a shared universal witness',r'A unary predicate $P$ is interpreted on an $n$-element nonempty labeled domain. Count interpretations satisfying $\exists xP(x)$ but not $\forall xP(x)$.',r'$2^n-2$',[r'$2^n-1$',r'$2^{n-1}$',r'$n$'],lambda n:2**n-2,[lambda n:2**n-1,lambda n:2**(n-1),lambda n:n],r'A predicate interpretation is a subset of the domain. Remove both the empty interpretation, which has no witness, and the whole domain, which makes the universal assertion true. These excluded subsets are distinct because the domain is nonempty.','On an empty domain, the existential is false and the universal is true.',['The existential becomes true on an empty domain.','A universal statement needs an existing witness to be true.','Every proper subset is a singleton.'],'Existence requires an element satisfying the predicate. Universal quantification is true when there is no counterexample, including an empty domain. Consequently the nonempty-domain derivation cannot subtract two distinct subsets in the empty case.')
f(t,'binary-total','Totality of a binary relation',r'A binary relation $R$ on an $n$-element labeled domain must satisfy $\forall x\exists yR(x,y)$. Count relation interpretations.',r'$(2^n-1)^n$',[r'$2^{n^2}-1$',r'$n^n$',r'$2^{n(n-1)}$'],lambda n:(2**n-1)**n,[lambda n:2**(n*n)-1,lambda n:n**n,lambda n:2**(n*(n-1))],r'View the relation as an $n$-by-$n$ Boolean matrix. Each row must be a nonempty subset of columns, giving $2^n-1$ choices per row. Rows are independently selectable. A nonempty matrix alone would not guarantee a witness in every row.','Exactly one witness per row changes the count to that of functions.',['At least one witness forces exactly one witness.','A single nonzero entry makes every row total.','Totality requires every column to be nonempty.'],'Replacing existence by unique existence restricts each row to a singleton. That yields $n$ choices per row and $n^n$ function graphs. Ordinary existence permits several related targets and must retain nonempty subsets of every size.')
f(t,'unique-witness','Exactly one relational target',r'Count relations on an $n$-element labeled domain satisfying: for every $x$, exactly one $y$ has $R(x,y)$.',r'$n^n$',[r'$(2^n-1)^n$',r'$n!$',r'$2^{n^2}$'],lambda n:n**n,[lambda n:(2**n-1)**n,lambda n:math.factorial(n),lambda n:2**(n*n)],r'Each argument selects exactly one of the $n$ targets. Selections at different arguments are independent, so the relation is the graph of an arbitrary function. No injectivity or column condition has been imposed.','Requiring distinct targets for distinct arguments gives a permutation.',['Unique existence alone implies injectivity.','Every function graph contains every ordered pair.','Two different arguments cannot share their witness.'],'Injectivity is a separate cross-row restriction. On equally sized finite domains, a function that is injective is a bijection, and its graph is a permutation matrix. Unique existence only guarantees one one-entry in each row.')
f(t,'canonical-parity','Parity and canonical clauses',r'A Boolean function on $n$ atoms is true exactly when the number of true atoms is odd. Count clauses in its canonical CNF.',r'$2^{n-1}$',[r'$2^n$',r'$n$',r'$2^{n-1}-1$'],lambda n:2**(n-1),[lambda n:2**n,lambda n:n,lambda n:2**(n-1)-1],r'Flip one specified input bit to pair each odd-parity row with a unique even-parity row. Half the truth-table rows are false. Canonical CNF uses one maxterm per false row, regardless of whether a more compact noncanonical formula exists.','Canonical size is different from minimum circuit size.',['A canonical maxterm corresponds to a true row.','A short XOR expression has only one canonical CNF clause.','Every parity row has the same output.'],'The canonical representation enumerates truth-table rows, while a circuit can share intermediate XOR computations. Thus an exponential canonical clause count does not imply that every circuit realizing parity must have exponentially many gates.')
f(t,'logical-consequence','Countermodels to a failed argument',r'For $n$ independently named atom pairs $(p_i,q_i)$, all premises $p_i\Rightarrow q_i$ must be true. The conclusion is $\bigwedge_i(q_i\Rightarrow p_i)$. Count premise-satisfying countermodels.',r'$3^n-2^n$',[r'$3^n$',r'$2^n$',r'$4^n-3^n$'],lambda n:3**n-2**n,[lambda n:3**n,lambda n:2**n,lambda n:4**n-3**n],r'Each forward implication permits three local states. The conjunction of both directions permits two local states, 00 and 11. Subtract the models also satisfying the conclusion from all premise models. A countermodel must make every premise true and the conclusion false.','One countermodel refutes validity; many supporting models cannot establish it.',['A false premise and false conclusion refute validity.','The converse is logically equivalent to the original implication.','Validity means that the conclusion is true in most premise models.'],'Validity universally quantifies over premise-satisfying interpretations. The state $p=0,q=1$ makes the forward implication true and the converse false; repeat this in any one pair to construct a genuine countermodel.')
f(t,'atleast-one-pair','Conjunctions inside a disjunction',r'A formula is $F=\bigvee_{i=1}^{n}(p_i\land q_i)$, with all $2n$ atoms distinct. Count satisfying assignments.',r'$4^n-3^n$',[r'$4^n$',r'$3^n$',r'$2^n$'],lambda n:4**n-3**n,[lambda n:4**n,lambda n:3**n,lambda n:2**n],r'Count the complement: every pair must avoid its unique 11 state, leaving three states per pair. Subtract these $3^n$ assignments from all $4^n$ assignments. Counting a chosen successful pair would count models with several successful pairs repeatedly.','The product argument fails when different pairs share atoms.',['Disjunction requires exactly one successful pair.','Pairs sharing an atom remain independent binary choices.','The false models are those with all atoms false.'],'Distinct variables make the local state spaces independent. If two clauses share a variable, selecting a state for one clause constrains the other. The complement method remains valid, but the simple product may need conditional counting.')
f(t,'functional-predicate','Truth patterns on equality classes',r'An equivalence relation on a domain has $n$ equivalence classes. A unary predicate must be constant on every class and true on at least one class. Count legal predicates.',r'$2^n-1$',[r'$2^n$',r'$n$',r'$2^{n-1}$'],lambda n:2**n-1,[lambda n:2**n,lambda n:n,lambda n:2**(n-1)],r'A class-constant predicate assigns one truth value to each class, regardless of how many elements the class contains. There are $2^n$ class assignments; remove the all-false assignment. The independent objects are classes rather than individual elements.','Unequal class sizes do not change this count.',['Every class must have the same size.','The count uses the number of ordered pairs in the relation.','The truth value is independently chosen for each element within a class.'],'Constancy couples all elements within the same class. Only the number of classes controls the number of independent truth-value choices; sizes matter for other questions, such as counting true elements, but not for this predicate count.')
f(t,'quantifier-negation','Negating a universally quantified row condition',r'On an $n$-element domain, count binary relations satisfying $\neg\forall x\exists yR(x,y)$.',r'$2^{n^2}-(2^n-1)^n$',[r'$(2^n-1)^n$',r'$2^{n^2}-1$',r'$n\cdot2^{n(n-1)}$'],lambda n:2**(n*n)-(2**n-1)**n,[lambda n:(2**n-1)**n,lambda n:2**(n*n)-1,lambda n:n*2**(n*(n-1))],r'The negation is $\exists x\forall y\neg R(x,y)$: at least one row is empty. Complement the relation matrices with no empty rows. Choosing a particular empty row and multiplying by the number of rows overcounts matrices with several empty rows.','Negation changes both quantifiers and the predicate.',['Negation changes only the predicate.','At least one empty row means that every row is empty.','Summing over possible empty rows always counts each matrix once.'],'De Morgan laws for quantifiers exchange universal and existential quantification at every scope boundary. The witness here identifies an empty row; it does not impose any restriction on other rows, so duplicate-witness overcounting must be avoided.')

t='d_sets'
f(t,'three-region','Element categories under containment',r'Count ordered pairs of subsets $(A,B)$ of an $n$-element labeled universe satisfying $A\subseteq B$.',r'$3^n$',[r'$4^n$',r'$2^n$',r'$n^3$'],lambda n:3**n,[lambda n:4**n,lambda n:2**n,lambda n:n**3],r'For each element, the legal memberships are outside both sets, in $B$ only, or in both. The state in $A$ but outside $B$ is forbidden. Multiply three choices over independent labeled elements.','Proper containment requires subtracting pairs with equal sets.',['Containment permits all four membership states.','Proper containment has exactly the same count as containment.','Every contained pair must have an empty smaller set.'],'Equal pairs have two choices per element and therefore number $2^n$. Subtracting them from the containment count gives $3^n-2^n$. Empty and equal pairs are distinct issues; proper containment excludes all equal pairs, not only the empty pair.')
f(t,'cover-two','Two sets whose union is prescribed',r'An $n$-element universe must equal $A\cup B$. Count ordered pairs $(A,B)$.',r'$3^n$',[r'$2^n$',r'$4^n$',r'$3^n-2^n$'],lambda n:3**n,[lambda n:2**n,lambda n:4**n,lambda n:3**n-2**n],r'Every element lies in $A$ only, $B$ only, or both. The outside-both state is excluded by the union condition. No disjointness condition forbids the both state, and the pair remains ordered.','Adding disjointness reduces each element to two possible memberships.',['Union coverage already guarantees disjointness.','The unordered count is always exactly half the ordered count.','An element may lie in neither set under full coverage.'],'If the sets must also be disjoint, each element belongs to exactly one of the two sets. For unordered pairs, symmetry must be analyzed with fixed points before dividing; the disjointness and labeling assumptions determine those fixed points.')
f(t,'chain-three','Nested families of sets',r'Count triples $A\subseteq B\subseteq C$ in an $n$-element universe.',r'$4^n$',[r'$8^n$',r'$3^n$',r'$2^n$'],lambda n:4**n,[lambda n:8**n,lambda n:3**n,lambda n:2**n],r'An element can enter the chain at $A$, at $B$, at $C$, or never enter. These four membership patterns exhaust the monotone possibilities; the other four arbitrary triple-membership patterns violate nesting.','For a chain of labeled sets, count entry positions rather than arbitrary bit patterns.',['Nesting allows all eight three-bit patterns.','All sets in a chain must be equal.','Each extra set in the chain doubles the number of element states.'],'A monotone chain has exactly one transition from absent to present per element. A chain with $k$ positions consequently has $k+1$ entry states, not $2^k$. Strictness between neighboring sets would add global witness requirements.')
f(t,'symmetric-fixed','Prescribed symmetric difference',r'Fix a subset $D$ in an $n$-element universe. Count ordered pairs with $A\triangle B=D$.',r'$2^n$',[r'$3^n$',r'$4^n$',r'$2^{n-1}$'],lambda n:2**n,[lambda n:3**n,lambda n:4**n,lambda n:2**(n-1)],r'Choose $A$ freely. The equation then forces $B=A\triangle D$. Equivalently each element has two permitted states: unequal memberships in $D$ and equal memberships outside $D$. There is no second independent set choice.','The map taking a set to its symmetric difference with a fixed set is a bijection.',['Symmetric difference with a fixed set is always idempotent.','A prescribed difference leaves both sets independent.','The number depends on the cardinality of the fixed set.'],r'Apply symmetric difference with $D$ twice to recover the original set because $D\triangle D$ is empty. This inverse proves bijectivity. Idempotence would require one application to equal two, which fails whenever the fixed set is nonempty.')
f(t,'proper-subsets','Nontrivial subsets and edge exclusions',r'Count nonempty proper subsets of an $n$-element nonempty set.',r'$2^n-2$',[r'$2^n-1$',r'$2^{n-1}$',r'$n$'],lambda n:2**n-2,[lambda n:2**n-1,lambda n:2**(n-1),lambda n:n],r'Use the power-set size, then exclude the empty set and the full set. These exclusions are disjoint for a nonempty universe. Do not confuse proper with merely nonempty, and do not replace the subset count by a singleton count.','The empty-universe case must be evaluated separately.',['The empty set is never a subset.','Every proper subset is nonempty.','Every nonempty subset is proper.'],'The empty set is a subset of every set, including itself. In an empty universe the empty set and the full set coincide, so subtracting two separate objects would be invalid; directly checking the requirements gives zero.')
f(t,'four-disjoint','Disjointness with three named subsets',r'Count ordered triples of pairwise disjoint subsets of an $n$-element universe; their union need not be the universe.',r'$4^n$',[r'$3^n$',r'$8^n$',r'$2^n$'],lambda n:4**n,[lambda n:3**n,lambda n:8**n,lambda n:2**n],r'Each element chooses one of the three subsets or the outside region. Pairwise disjointness rules out membership in two or three subsets. Because outside elements are allowed, the extra fourth category must be retained.','Requiring full union removes exactly the outside category.',['Pairwise disjointness requires full union.','Having an empty three-way intersection is sufficient for pairwise disjointness.','Every subset must be nonempty under pairwise disjointness.'],'Pairwise disjointness prohibits every two-way intersection, a stronger requirement than empty triple intersection. Full coverage is an additional constraint; it changes the independent element-state count from four to three, while nonemptiness needs inclusion-exclusion.')
f(t,'fixed-intersection','Pairs with a known intersection',r'A universe has $2n$ elements. Fix an $n$-element subset $I$ and require $A\cap B=I$. Count ordered pairs.',r'$3^n$',[r'$4^n$',r'$2^n$',r'$3^{2n}$'],lambda n:3**n,[lambda n:4**n,lambda n:2**n,lambda n:3**(2*n)],r'Elements of $I$ must lie in both sets, so they contribute no choice. Every remaining element has three states, outside both or in exactly one set; the both state is forbidden because the intersection is already fixed.','The prescribed intersection fixes membership inside that intersection.',['Elements of the fixed intersection may belong to just one set.','The remaining elements are forced outside both sets.','The intersection cardinality alone fixes both sets uniquely.'],'The requirement is equality of sets, not merely a lower bound on an intersection. Inside the prescribed set both memberships are forced; outside it common membership is forbidden, but either exclusive membership remains legal.')
f(t,'complement-pairs','Complementary pairs',r'Count ordered pairs satisfying $A\cap B=\varnothing$ and $A\cup B=U$ for an $n$-element universe.',r'$2^n$',[r'$3^n$',r'$4^n$',r'$2^n-1$'],lambda n:2**n,[lambda n:3**n,lambda n:4**n,lambda n:2**n-1],r'Together the two conditions force $B=U\setminus A$. Choosing $A$ determines the pair. Either empty set is permitted because the statement imposes neither nonemptiness nor properness.','Both intersection and union conditions are necessary to identify complements.',['Disjointness alone proves that one set is the complement of the other.','Full union alone proves complementarity.','A set cannot be its own complement in an empty universe.'],'Disjointness without coverage permits elements in neither set; coverage without disjointness permits elements in both. For the empty universe the empty set is its own relative complement, so unusual boundary cases must be tested directly.')

t='d_proof'
f(t,'square-divisibility','A converse with a hidden factor',r'Among integers $1,\ldots,6n$, count integers $x$ for which $6\mid x^2$.',r'$n$',[r'$2n$',r'$3n$',r'$6n$'],lambda n:n,[lambda n:2*n,lambda n:3*n,lambda n:6*n],r'Because 2 and 3 are prime, each divides $x^2$ exactly when it divides $x$. Thus $6$ divides the square exactly when $6$ divides $x$. The interval contains precisely the multiples 6,12 through $6n$.','The same argument for arbitrary composite moduli requires their prime exponents.',['Every divisor of a square divides its root.','If 4 divides a square, 4 must divide its root.','Divisibility of the root by a modulus never implies divisibility of its square.'],'Prime divisibility passes from a square to the base. Composite divisibility does not in general: 4 divides the square of 2 although it does not divide 2. For a modulus, compare each required prime exponent with twice the exponent in the base.')
f(t,'pigeonhole-threshold','Forcing a large occupancy',r'Distribute labeled objects into $n$ boxes. Find the smallest object count forcing a box to contain at least four objects.',r'$3n+1$',[r'$3n$',r'$4n$',r'$n+4$'],lambda n:3*n+1,[lambda n:3*n,lambda n:4*n,lambda n:n+4],r'If every box contains at most three objects, the total is at most $3n$. One more object contradicts that bound. Sharpness is proved by placing exactly three objects in each box at the preceding total.','A sharp threshold requires both a forcing proof and a construction below it.',['A contradiction proof alone always establishes minimality.','The average occupancy must equal four before the conclusion is forced.','The boxes must be nonempty for the upper-capacity argument.'],'The capacity bound proves sufficiency of one more than the maximum nonforcing total. Minimality comes from an actual distribution at the preceding total. Empty boxes do not break an upper-capacity estimate; allowing them only gives more possible nonforcing arrangements.')
f(t,'subset-comparable','A sharp pair-selection bound',r'Choose integers from $\{1,\ldots,2n\}$. Find the smallest number forcing two chosen numbers to sum to $2n+1$.',r'$n+1$',[r'$n$',r'$2n$',r'$n+2$'],lambda n:n+1,[lambda n:n,lambda n:2*n,lambda n:n+2],r'Partition the universe into the $n$ complementary pairs $(i,2n+1-i)$. Choosing more than one number per pair forces a complete pair. Choosing one member of each pair constructs a nonforcing set of size $n$.','Selecting with repetition would change the meaning of two chosen numbers.',['The conclusion already holds for every set of size the number of pairs.','A proof needs all pairs to have the same chosen member.','The complementary pairs overlap.'],'The pairing proof uses a genuine partition of distinct available integers. The largest counterexample chooses one endpoint per pair. If repetitions are permitted, the pigeonhole witness can be repeated copies of one endpoint rather than both complementary values.')
f(t,'prime-products','Counterexample counting with a modular predicate',r'Among integers $1,\ldots,6n$, count numbers divisible by 2 or 3 but not by 6.',r'$3n$',[r'$4n$',r'$2n$',r'$5n$'],lambda n:3*n,[lambda n:4*n,lambda n:2*n,lambda n:5*n],r'There are $3n$ even numbers and $2n$ multiples of 3. Their intersection has $n$ multiples of 6. Inclusion-exclusion gives $4n$ in the union; exclude the intersection once more to get $3n$ in the exclusive union.','The set of multiples of 2 or 3 is not closed under addition.',['Every union of additive subgroups is an additive subgroup.','Being divisible by one prime forces divisibility by their product.','Inclusion-exclusion adds the intersection rather than subtracting it.'],'The witnesses 2 and 3 lie in the union, but their sum 5 does not. This refutes closure directly. Union counting and algebraic closure are separate claims; a correct cardinality calculation cannot prove a subgroup property.')
f(t,'strict-order-countermodels','Testing an existential claim by its witnesses',r'For ordered pairs $(x,y)$ drawn from $\{1,\ldots,n\}$, count counterexamples to the claim $x\le y$.',r'$n(n-1)/2$',[r'$n(n+1)/2$',r'$n^2$',r'$n-1$'],lambda n:n*(n-1)//2,[lambda n:n*(n+1)//2,lambda n:n*n,lambda n:n-1],r'A counterexample must satisfy the negation $x>y$. For each $x$, there are $x-1$ smaller targets. Sum the integers from zero to $n-1$, or pair the two orientations of each distinct unordered pair.','Negating a non-strict inequality produces a strict inequality.',['The negation of a non-strict inequality includes equality.','A witness satisfying the claim refutes it.','One failed numerical example proves every pair violates the claim.'],'Equality satisfies the original claim, so diagonal pairs are not counterexamples. One strict reverse pair refutes a universally asserted inequality but does not establish that the opposite inequality holds for all pairs; quantifier scope remains essential.')
f(t,'residue-square','Eliminating impossible residues',r'Among residues modulo $2n$, count residues $r$ for which $r^2$ is odd.',r'$n$',[r'$2n$',r'$n-1$',r'$n+1$'],lambda n:n,[lambda n:2*n,lambda n:n-1,lambda n:n+1],r'Reduce the parity of the base: even squares are even and odd squares are odd. A complete residue system modulo an even modulus contains exactly half odd residues. No evaluation of every large square is required.','Necessary residue conditions alone need not prove existence.',['An integer is a square whenever its residue is compatible with squares.','Odd squares can arise from even bases.','A congruence contradiction can never refute an integer equation.'],'Residue obstructions prove impossibility when a required residue cannot occur. Passing a residue test is only necessary in general: many nonsquares share residues with squares. A proof of existence still needs a construction or a sufficient theorem.')
f(t,'contrapositive-count','Counting the failure of an implication',r'For $n$ independent pairs of Boolean assertions, count assignments making every implication true but at least one antecedent false.',r'$3^n-1$',[r'$3^n$',r'$2^n$',r'$4^n-1$'],lambda n:3**n-1,[lambda n:3**n,lambda n:2**n,lambda n:4**n-1],r'All implications give three allowed pair states each. The only such assignment with every antecedent true also has every consequent true. Exclude that single assignment from the $3^n$ premise models.','A false antecedent satisfies a material implication without proving its consequent.',['A false antecedent makes an implication false.','A true implication proves its consequent unconditionally.','The inverse is always equivalent to the implication.'],'Material implication is false only with a true antecedent and false consequent. Modus ponens additionally requires the antecedent; a vacuously true implication does not supply it. The contrapositive, rather than the converse or inverse, preserves logical equivalence.')
f(t,'distance-triangle','A bounded-search counterexample space',r'Count triples $(a,b,c)$ of positive integers with $a+b+c=n+4$.',r'$\binom{n+3}{2}$',[r'$\binom{n+4}{2}$',r'$\binom{n+2}{2}$',r'$(n+4)^2$'],lambda n:C(n+3,2),[lambda n:C(n+4,2),lambda n:C(n+2,2),lambda n:(n+4)**2],r'Subtract one from each positive variable. The new nonnegative total is $n+1$ over three variables. Stars and bars gives two separator positions among $n+3$ places. This parameterization explicitly enumerates the permitted search domain.','Exhausting a finite domain proves only the bounded statement searched.',['A finite search proves every unbounded integer statement.','Positive and nonnegative domains are interchangeable.','A counterexample outside the domain refutes the bounded statement.'],'The quantifier must match the searched domain. A complete check over one bounded total is a proof for that total, whereas an unbounded claim needs a general argument. A genuine counterexample must satisfy every stated positivity and total-sum condition.')

t='d_induction'
f(t,'weighted-geometric','A sum whose induction step has a boundary term',r'Evaluate $\sum_{k=1}^{n}k2^{k-1}$.',r'$(n-1)2^n+1$',[r'$n2^n$',r'$(n+1)2^n-1$',r'$2^n-1$'],lambda n:(n-1)*2**n+1,[lambda n:n*2**n,lambda n:(n+1)*2**n-1,lambda n:2**n-1],r'The base case gives one. Assuming the formula at $n$, add $(n+1)2^n$; the two coefficient terms combine to $n2^{n+1}$ and the constant one remains. This establishes the stated formula at the next index.','An induction step must preserve the constant boundary term.',['The first numerical example is sufficient without the induction step.','The induction hypothesis may assume the target next case.','Changing the lower summation limit never affects the constant term.'],'The accumulated constant encodes the starting boundary. Dropping it yields a plausible asymptotic estimate but an incorrect exact identity. A proof must state the first valid index and derive the successor from the preceding valid assertion.')
f(t,'squares','A polynomial sum with a nontrivial successor',r'Evaluate $\sum_{k=1}^{n}k^2$.',r'$n(n+1)(2n+1)/6$',[r'$n(n+1)/2$',r'$n^3$',r'$n(n+1)(n+2)/6$'],lambda n:n*(n+1)*(2*n+1)//6,[lambda n:n*(n+1)//2,lambda n:n**3,lambda n:n*(n+1)*(n+2)//6],r'Assume the cubic expression for the first $n$ squares and add $(n+1)^2$. Factor out $n+1$ and simplify the remainder to $(n+2)(2n+3)/6$, the required next-index expression. The base case is immediate but necessary.','Factoring the difference of successive closed forms can prove the same identity.',['The exact sum equals its leading cubic term.','The induction hypothesis can omit all parameter restrictions.','A polynomial identity holds because its degree is small.'],'If a proposed closed form has the correct initial value and the correct difference at each successive integer, it follows by telescoping or induction. Leading terms alone provide asymptotic information and cannot establish an exact finite sum.')
f(t,'binomial-weights','A combinatorial identity and its inductive interpretation',r'Evaluate $\sum_{k=0}^{n}k\binom nk$.',r'$n2^{n-1}$',[r'$2^n$',r'$n2^n$',r'$n!$'],lambda n:n*2**(n-1),[lambda n:2**n,lambda n:n*2**n,lambda n:math.factorial(n)],r'Count pairs consisting of a subset and one distinguished member. Choose the member in $n$ ways and choose any subset of the other $n-1$ elements. The algebraic version uses $k\binom nk=n\binom{n-1}{k-1}$ before summing.','For the zero-size case, evaluate the original sum rather than treating a negative exponent as a count.',['Every subset has exactly one distinguished-member choice.','The empty subset contributes one to this weighted sum.','Counting the same pairs in two orders is invalid.'],'The empty subset contributes zero because it has no distinguished member. The weighted count tracks subset-member pairs, not just subsets. The zero case equals zero directly, while the derivation with a selected member assumes a positive domain size.')
f(t,'full-binary','Strong induction on a tree constructor',r'A full binary tree has $n$ internal nodes. Find its total node count.',r'$2n+1$',[r'$n+1$',r'$2n$',r'$2^n-1$'],lambda n:2*n+1,[lambda n:n+1,lambda n:2*n,lambda n:2**n-1],r'Every internal node has two outgoing edges, so there are $2n$ edges. A finite tree has one more node than edges. Equivalently combine the identities for two subtrees and one new root; structural induction keeps the counts synchronized.','Full does not mean perfect or balanced.',['Every full binary tree has logarithmic height.','Every internal node in a full binary tree has one child.','The node count determines a unique tree shape.'],'Fullness constrains the number of children at internal nodes, while balance and perfection constrain depth. A full tree can be highly skewed. The edge-count identity therefore gives the node total without fixing height or shape.')
f(t,'telescoping','Reciprocal terms with a telescoping induction step',r'Evaluate $\sum_{k=1}^{n}1/(k(k+1))$.',r'$n/(n+1)$',[r'$1/(n+1)$',r'$n/(n+2)$',r'$n$'],lambda n:F(n,n+1),[lambda n:F(1,n+1),lambda n:F(n,n+2),lambda n:n],r'Write each term as $1/k-1/(k+1)$. All interior terms cancel, leaving $1-1/(n+1)$. The induction proof adds the next reciprocal product and obtains the same next-index fraction after combining denominators.','Cancellation is justified for finite sums before taking a limit.',['A divergent difference of infinite series can always be canceled termwise.','Every term of a telescoping sum is zero.','The first and last boundary terms also cancel.'],'The finite partial sum is exactly defined and its surviving boundaries are visible. Only after obtaining that expression should a limit be taken. Rearranging divergent infinite sums as if they were finite can change or destroy meaning.')
f(t,'hanoi','A recurrence with one central operation',r'The minimum transfer recurrence is $H_0=0$ and $H_n=2H_{n-1}+1$. Find $H_n$.',r'$2^n-1$',[r'$2^n$',r'$n^2$',r'$2^{n-1}-1$'],lambda n:2**n-1,[lambda n:2**n,lambda n:n*n,lambda n:2**(n-1)-1],r'Assume the closed form at the preceding index. Doubling gives $2^n-2$ and the central operation restores one, producing $2^n-1$. Alternatively add one to the recurrence and obtain a pure doubling relation.','Proving a construction has this cost does not alone prove optimality.',['Any recursive construction is automatically minimal.','The central operation can be omitted without changing the exact count.','Induction cannot prove inequalities.'],'An upper bound comes from a feasible construction. A minimum claim also needs a lower bound showing that the two subtransfers and the central move are unavoidable. Induction verifies the recurrence solution after that modeling argument is established.')
f(t,'ternary-tree','Constructor accounting for a full ternary tree',r'A rooted full ternary tree has $n$ internal nodes. Find its number of leaves.',r'$2n+1$',[r'$3n+1$',r'$n+1$',r'$2n$'],lambda n:2*n+1,[lambda n:3*n+1,lambda n:n+1,lambda n:2*n],r'There are $3n$ edges. If the leaf count is $L$, the node-edge identity gives $n+L=3n+1$, hence $L=2n+1$. Structural induction adds three subtree identities and accounts for the new internal root.','The identity requires exactly three children at every internal node.',['Allowing one-child nodes preserves the identity unchanged.','A root leaf is excluded from the base case.','The leaf count depends only on tree height.'],'Changing the branching rule changes edge accounting. A one-child chain has many internal nodes but only one leaf. The single root leaf is the valid zero-internal-node base case and is necessary for the structural proof.')
f(t,'nested-recurrence','Exact totals for a prefix recurrence',r'Let $a_0=1$ and $a_n=2+\sum_{j=0}^{n-1}a_j$ for $n\ge1$. Calculate $a_n$.',r'$3\cdot2^{n-1}$',[r'$2^n$',r'$3^n$',r'$2^{n+1}-1$'],lambda n:3*2**(n-1),[lambda n:2**n,lambda n:3**n,lambda n:2**(n+1)-1],r'The first term after the boundary is three. Subtract consecutive recurrence equations to obtain $a_{n+1}=2a_n$ for positive indices. Strong induction can also sum all earlier geometric terms. The special initial value must not be replaced by a backward extrapolation.','Subtracting neighboring equations can reduce the order while retaining a special first step.',['The formula must give the same value at every omitted initial index.','A prefix sum always grows polynomially.','Strong induction assumes every future case.'],'The recurrence starts with a separate index-zero value. The derived doubling relation is valid only after the first positive term is established; using it across the exceptional boundary would produce an incorrect seed and every later term would inherit that error.')
